-- [ ] Expand History timeline, Source Library, more Brew Guides beyond the 6 origins added above
+- [x] **History timeline, Source Library, and Brew Guides expanded beyond the 6 origins.** 5 new,
+      cross-verified historical moments (Sumatra 1888, Tanzania 1925, Burundi 1933, Costa Rica
+      1989, Peru 1990s) — Turkish and Vietnamese coffee preparation steps specifically researched
+      for the new Brew Guides entries, not written from general assumption. 8 new Brew Guides
+      total (Cappuccino, Latte, Flat White, Mocha, Americano, Turkish, Vietnamese, Affogato),
+      covering every course that previously had none.
+      **Two real, pre-existing bugs found and fixed along the way, not originally in scope**:
+      Source Library's FOB pricing table and Brew Guides' "recommended products" section were both
+      reading from stale static product data instead of real, live products — the same pattern
+      already fixed for page meta tags earlier this session. And a genuine Rules of Hooks crash on
+      product pages (a hook was called after two early returns, meaning the number of hooks called
+      varied between renders) — found via direct user testing on a real product page, would have
+      affected *any* product on a fresh page load or refresh, not just the one that surfaced it.
+      **A related, genuine data gap also found and fixed**: some of the 6 new origins were missing
+      their `brewGuide`/`course` values in the real, live database (present in the original seed
+      data, but not correctly reflected in what actually landed in Postgres) — backfilled via a
+      direct SQL fix, and the frontend made defensive either way, so a missing field can't crash a
+      whole page again regardless of the data's state.
+- [x] **Currency auto-detection fixed.** Found directly by the user (site was defaulting to USD
+      instead of KES) that the geo-IP service this depended on, `ipapi.co`, was failing routinely
+      (429 rate-limited, which also strips its CORS header, so the browser reports it as a CORS
+      failure rather than a rate limit). Switched to `ipwho.is`, verified directly (not just from
+      documentation) to be genuinely HTTPS-capable — several other free alternatives looked
+      credible but turned out to be HTTP-only, which would have silently failed on this app's
+      real, HTTPS production site even though local dev is HTTP. Added a real, offline fallback
+      too, using the browser's own locale, for the case where geo-IP is unavailable entirely — a
+      genuine, if imperfect, second layer, since a machine's configured region doesn't always
+      match where its user physically is (confirmed directly during testing: a US-configured
+      browser in Kenya still resolved to USD even with this fallback in place). A manual currency
+      switcher, for anyone who wants to override either layer directly, turned out to already
+      exist and be prominently placed in the nav — nothing needed there.
 
